amples of Gandhi using a \"hostile\" literary work to fuel his own social reform movement.\nUnlike his other interactions, this was a battle of pens. X was an American researcher and journalist who visited India in the 1920s and published the bombshell book \"_____ _____\" in 1927.\nWhen the book was released, it caused an international scandal. Many Indians wanted to ban it, but Gandhi took a different approach. He wrote a famous review of the book in his journal Young India, titled \"The Drain Inspector's Report.\"\nGandhi compared X to a \"drain inspector\" who comes to a beautiful city but only looks at its sewers. He didn't say the sewers didn't exist; he just argued that X had ignored the rest of the city.", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}]